[Music]  Americans are increasingly connected to  the virtual world through phones  computers and smart appliances  now even vital utilities like power and  water grids are going digital only on  CBS this morning we got access to a  massive power plant near Niagara Falls  New York  it's upgrading its systems with  artificial intelligence that is raising  concerns among some security experts  about the dangers from hackers  Errol Barnett shows us how power plants  can defend themselves good morning  cybersecurity firms are tracking an  increased number of attempted attacks on  US infrastructure with one identifying  at least eight groups of state-sponsored  hackers meanwhile our homes are linked  to the grid through smart thermostats  and other connected devices so to see  what the risks are I explored New York  State's high-tech utility infrastructure  which is now becoming more reliant on  artificial intelligence to see how our  grid is protected from attack we are now  layering new technologies to this system  that was built back in the 1960s  old-school hydropower is getting a  new-school upgrade thanks to artificial  intelligence New York State's largest  power plant harnesses the awesome energy  of the Niagara River and now the New  York Power Authority or Napa is  connecting this plant and miles of  transmission lines with tens of  thousands of sensors which can  essentially think so if there are  anomalies or if the temperature is  outside the range of design this sensors  will communicate automatically to our  integrated smart Operations Center the  AI network reaches statewide warning not  only of problems on the grid but  predicting where unscheduled maintenance  is needed the aim is to lower costs and  to pass savings on to customers with all  data processed and verified here by  computer engineers with the improvements  of software and computing power you also  have the increase in potential risks  from cyberattacks how do you protect  against that absolutely  we have to think about cyber at every  step of the process we have to  incorporate cyber defenses in every step  of the software and hardware that we're  putting together there's more risk than  we've ever had before Rob Lee spent most  of his military career at the National  Security Agency and now runs his own  consulting firm after sounding the alarm  about weaknesses in the u.s. grid to  Congress he is critical of States  relying on AI systems and computer  networks in general the communication  paths that a house might open a path for  adversary to take advantage of as well  Lee led the US military's investigation  into the Russian hack of the Ukrainian  power grid in 2015 made possible he says  by a simple internet connection they  opened up circuit breakers and  de-energize portions of grid leaving  225,000 customers or so in the darkness  for a couple hours Lee warns AI without  highly-skilled humans involved is risky  what are the vulnerabilities it opens up  you don't want your grid operators that  humans that are controlling your grid to  become so dependent on the machine  learning or AI model that they forget  how to do their jobs  these systems are thinking they're  making decisions what do you say to  people who are concerned about that  there are still humans in between the  information and the actions so think of  this really as a very smart decision  support system now we weren't allowed to  show you but Gil tells me their power  plant control rooms have safeguards and  operate on a completely different  network from the rest of the grid still  the Department of Homeland Security  revealed earlier this year  Russian malware was opened by someone in  a power plant control room via email it  was as simple as that  and that's why you have this growing  consensus that it is critical to  increase the skills of everyone plugged  into the network Anthony thanks Errol

